keep visualizer is a visualization tool  that helps you analyze the excess  patterns in your cloud BigTable instance  the cloud BigTable console lets you  monitor your application with line  charts which is effective in most cases  however how would you know what is  causing high CPU if there are any  hotspots or large rows or if a MapReduce  is affecting your serving latency that's  why we are announcing key visualizer for  cloud BigTable it shows you read and  write patterns in two dimensions over  time and across key space it helps you  find or prevent hotspots it helps you  find rows with too much data which can  cause performance issues and you can see  if your key schema is balanced key  visualizer has a heat map that shows  time horizontally in this example we see  the number of operations over one week  the key space is shown vertically a heat  scale shows values of a selected metric  for example opps from cold representing  zero over blue to purple to red to  yellow to glowing white for very high  values the tooltip will show us details  about this metric for particular time  and key range in this example notice  that some key ranges like the one at the  bottom exhibit a diurnal pattern whereas  other ranges are very cold  also notice the white glowing line in  the middle that shows the busiest key  range the diagonal lines represent  linear scans possibly from a MapReduce  job the bar chart at the bottom shows  average values across all visible keys  in 15-minute intervals  similarly the bar chart on the right  shows the averages over time per key  range this peak again shows our busiest  key BigTable developers often create  keys with a prefix hierarchy in this  example we see keys that are constructed  with DES limiters such as slashes or  dashes key visualizer has a heuristic  that automatically extracts the prefix  tree from your key schema if available  the key prefix hierarchy is shown on the  left side we can see that the behavior  of a table often correlates with the key  prefixes as in this example when we  click on a prefix it will zoom into the  corresponding key range we can also zoom  and pan by using the scroll wheel or  dragging key visualizer offers you these  11 metrics UPS is the sum of read and  write operations the three warning  metrics read or write pressure index and  large rows can discover problem areas  then we have read metrics right metrics  latency and the amount of data stored  key visualizer  automatically analyzes the performance  of your BigTable instance this may  involve trillions of metric values not  only does key visualizer aggregate all  these metric values to visualize them in  the browser it also performs a detailed  analysis on your tables performance this  produces the following metrics read  pressure which is an opaque function of  Reed CPU read queue length which is the  number of read ops waiting to be  executed and latency write pressure is a  similar function to detect bad  performance for writing and larger as'  which will flag any row that contains  more than one gigabyte of data the  result shown in this view lets us  discover and prevent hotspots best of  all these computations won't cause any  performance degradation on your table  and all this is free let's now see how  we made this easy for you key visualizer  opens by default with the ops metric  this gives us an overview of the  activity in your table then you check if  there are any warnings at the top bar if  there aren't that's good news your  tables performance is most likely fine  this example has three warnings one of  them is severe as indicated by the three  exclamation marks  clicking on the read pressure index  warning brings us to the read pressure  metric where we see immediately where  and when the problem happened  we now zoom into the white glowing  stripe the tooltip shows us which keys  are causing problems at the top we see  the worst problem it's a single key that  has been stressed for more than three  hours we can see the precise name of the  offending key we can see that it was  using nearly 600 million nodes worth of  CPU which is more than half a server CPU  and it tells us what caused this bad  performance this row contains nine point  twenty two gigabytes of data which is  way more than the recommended 256  megabytes per row in all these examples  we were looking at one metric at a time  very often however we want to compare  different metrics rather than switching  between metrics we can show all the  metrics and their values together on the  right when we expand these we can see  all these metrics and miniature views  the zoom and pan operations are  synchronized so we could compare details  for each metric key visualizer for cloud  BigTable visualizes the key access  patterns in your table helps you find  hotspots and large rows it extracts the  key prefix hierarchy if available key  visualizer analyzes trillions of metric  values to point you to problem areas and  it does this without any impact on your  cloud BigTable performance  [Music]
 

